  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Forecast remains on track today with surface high \ncentered near the coast this afternoon. This has brought yet \nanother pleasant afternoon to ENC as the area remains under partly \ncloudy skies, east to southeasterly winds, low humidity, and temps \nin the mid to upper 80s inland and mid 70s to low 80s along the \ncoast and OBX. \n\nAloft, upper level trough that was over the Mid-Atlantic today will \ngradually push offshore on Thursday and will continue east as \nupper level ridging replaces it. At the surface, high pressure \ncurrently centered along the coast will continue to push out to \nsea tonight into Thurs allowing SE'rly winds to veer further to \na S-SW'rly direction by Thurs night. Prior to this occuring, \nexpect winds to once again become light and variable to calm \ntonight across ENC. With clear skies, ample low level moisture, \nand light winds we have a threat for more widespread fog \ntonight with the potential for visibilities below 1 mile at \ntimes given the setup. REFS and HREF data suggest A 50-70% \nchance for visibilities below 1 mile across most of ENC outside\nof the far norther tier of counties and the OBX. As a result \nhave a mix of patchy and widespread fog in the grids tonight \nafter midnight across much of ENC. Lows this evening are \nforecast to remain in the 60s. Beyond tonight, upper ridging \nwill be over the Mid-Atlantic into the end of the week before \npushing offshore with the approach of an upper level trough in \nthe Ohio River Valley that will push east as well. At the \nsurface, high pressure will be centered offshore keeping S'rly \nflow in place across ENC and allowing for dry weather and a \ncontinued warming trend into the end of the week. By the end of \nthe week the area could flirt with record highs given the set \nup (SEE CLIMATE SECTION FOR MORE DETAILS). \n\nFor reference, normal highs in early October are around 80 degrees, \nand forecast highs are in the mid to upper 80s for much of ENC, with \nsome 90s possible inland Thurs, likely low 90s on Fri. \nPersistent southerly flow and increasing low- level moisture \nshould help to keep overnight lows higher as well, and well \nabove the normal lows of around 60.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...No real changes in forecast thinking this weekend \nand into next week as precipitation chances return for the weekend \nas Gulf moisture opens up and a cold front drapes across the \nsouthern Appalachians and pushes southeastward slowly on Saturday. \nThis will squeeze moisture into eastern NC elevating rain chances \nfor the weekend. While thunderstorms will be possible, the threat \nfor severe weather is low at this time.\n\nThe front loses steam as it approaches the coast, potentially \nbriefly stalling over Coastal Carolinas, ahead of a second \nreinforcing front spurred from the Great Lakes by a stronger trough \naloft. Precip chances do look to diminish by Tue next week as the \naforementioned fronts push offshore and a strong high pressure \nridge builds in from the north and west.\n\nFor now, ensemble guidance is showing about a 30-50% chance of \nexceeding 1\" of rain with this system. Any rain will be helpful, \nespecially for the coastal plain of ENC that is currently in \nmoderate to extreme drought.",
